[Music]  so you found yourself a super-duper  hidden secret dungeon now did you well  good on you  time to go dungeon crawling oh but wait  yeah we're not talking about dungeon  exploration today but rather something  equally important preparation most of  the common complaints about breath the  wild I can totally get behind or at the  very least understand it from their  perspective I can see why people were  put off by the weapon durability system  I agree that the story could have been  more fleshed out and that the shrines  got repetitive after one hundred and  twenty iterations and I'm all in favor  of breath wah to not taking place during  monsoon season there's one complaint I  do have a particular gripe with though  it's not the most prominent of  criticisms but it's definitely out there  here's a very basic reenactment of that  complaint so I was in this shrine right  and I ran out of arrows which I needed  to progress so I had to leave the shrine  and go all the way to the shop to buy  some more I wanted to skip time and wait  for the rain to stop but I didn't have  any firewood or flint on me which was so  frustrating well guess what that's on  you buddy  breath of the wild is a game about  survival managing your resources and  equipment well you just want everything  handed to you on a silver platter I am  of course over exaggerating but I do  stand by the fact that preparing for a  challenge is half the fun it makes that  challenge all the more satisfying to  complete as opposed to the older  installments in the series there's no  limit to the amount of ammunition you  can carry so in that regard there's no  excuse not to make sure to have a decent  stock of arrows on you at all time the  same goes for other items that might  come in handy at any point me I always  make sure to have a torch and a coral  leaf on me it doesn't matter how good of  a weapon I may find I will just drop a  weaker one I try to make sure I have at  least a few different elemental weapons  on me just in case I run into an enemy  who's weak to that element especially  fire based weapons come in handy in case  I need to start an easy campfire light a  torch or burn an obstacle sure it sucks  having to sacrifice a valuable weapons  law just in case you need that one  specific thing to progress and Nintendo  might be wise to give these types of  items there  separate category instead of pooling  them all together with the other weapons  nonetheless it was a sacrifice I was  willing to make during my first  playthrough of the game call me old  school but I really feel like modern  video games have spoiled us a little too  much at times just a sheer thought that  a player might be annoyed or frustrated  to have to turn back and resupply has  driven developers to make sure they hand  us all the stuff we need right on the  spot at least in terms of the Zelda  series this approach has one unfortunate  side-effect namely giving away the  solution prematurely and sacrificing  responsible resource management for  convenience the early entries in the 3d  installments are particularly guilty of  this where the game designers purposely  place a specific item or ammunition in a  room just so we don't have to leave the  dungeon to restock what this essentially  does is hint that the solution before we  even have to think about it oh look it's  an endlessly respawning bush containing  Deku seeds  I guess there's probably a nice witch or  something I got a hit ah Deku Baba guess  I'm gonna need that stick tool at me  some fire in this room gee they are sure  handing out a lot of magic right before  I enter this door I'm guessing the boss  will probably be weak to magic attacks  now I cannot completely fault the older  games for doing this since as mentioned  before ammunition was capped at a  relatively low number so it was much  easier to run out of resources but it's  definitely not something I would like to  see repeated in the future with the  sequel to breath of the wild we will no  longer be restricted to the old system  which only let you carry so much stuff  at a time instead of placing jars around  with the necessary stuff inside I'm in  favor of giving that responsibility back  to the player if you didn't prepare a  decent amount of meals or potions in  advance with different status effects  and refills before you venture into a  dungeon you have no one to blame but  yourself the early 2d games who are a  lot more ruthless in that regard  ruthless but not completely unfair you  still found stuff you might need later  on in the dungeon but not necessarily in  the same room or right before a boss  fight in a Link to the Past it was  pretty much mandatory to carry at least  one or two bottles of magic potions with  you if you wanted to stand a chance at  clearing Turtle Rock or the ice palace I  do understand that Nintendo one Zelda to  be accessible for players of different  skill levels and breath of the wild -  definitely shouldn't be notoriously hard  Akins  Ultima Underworld when it comes to  dungeon crawling but a good challenge  shouldn't be avoided either I've  compiled a small list of things I think  would make for a nice inclusion in the  sequel when it comes to dungeon  preparation both in terms of items but  also gameplay mechanics keep in mind  these are just things I would like to  see they are not predictions when it  came to lighting your way in the dark  place the torches did the trick  however as mentioned before the big turn  noun was the fact that it hawks your  weapon slots I think a lantern similar  to the one from Twilight Princess would  make a welcome return this item would  consume lantern oil just like in the  afro mentioned game but it wouldn't take  up space in your inventory the lantern  oil itself could function the same way  as any of the other collectibles we find  in the game something we can either mine  or buy and hey link uses cooking oil  when he's preparing food so why not 