f you think about it how did  all these other adventurers obtain their  gear now as I mentioned in my breath of  the wild milestone video the weapon  durability system didn't really bother  me that much but if we're going to have  large and complex dungeons or caves to  explore in the sequel this will become a  problem finding an entrance to a dungeon  only to realize half of your inventory  is either empty or damaged is not  exactly ideal since we would have to  spend quite some time randomly scouring  a landscape just to look for some decent  weapons it would be best if we have the  option to buy or repair them just in  case the Master Sword decides to bug out  on us again shops could be limited to  only selling low to mid-level gear  enough to keep us going but without  taking away the exploration part of  discovering rare and powerful weaponry  in the overworld or high-level weapons  could simply come at a very hefty price  alternatively we could be able to repair  certain weapons by sacrificing materials  instead of just gemstones we could for  instance be able to mine precious metals  gold silver copper etc the stronger the  weapon the more rare and expensive the  materials you would have to sacrifice  for a quick fix  Guardian based weapons would be repaired  with ancient parts wooden weapons with  wooden fiber you get the point  this way we can still have weapon  durability to a certain degree but also  options to restock on weapons more  easily for means of preparing for a  serious dungeon crawl being able to  craft certain items on the spot given  you have the right materials would be an  amazing addition to the game in my  opinion this doesn't have to be as  complex as in other games of course with  tons and tons of options skill levels  and combinations this isn't an RPG but I  don't see why link shouldn't be able to  craft some basic necessities suppose the  devs decide to skip my lantern idea and  simply stick to the torches if say I  have a bundle of 